{"id":22,"course_id":9,"title":"Pending Pods, Scheduling, and Node Pressure","summary":"Read scheduler messages instead of guessing why a Pod will not start.","sequence":3,"body_simplified":"## Pending means not placed or not ready to start\nA Pending Pod may be unscheduled, waiting for volumes, blocked by image pulls, or constrained by node selectors, taints, or resource requests. The scheduler tells you a lot in events.\n- Insufficient CPU or memory means requests do not fit current nodes.\n- Untolerated taint means the Pod is not allowed on matching nodes.\n- Node selector or affinity can make the Pod too picky.\n- PVC binding can block stateful Pods before the container starts.\n\n## Inspection commands\n$ kubectl describe pod pending-pod -n payments\n$ kubectl get nodes\n$ kubectl describe node NODE_NAME\n$ kubectl get pvc -n payments\nLook for `0/3 nodes are available`, taints, node affinity, volume binding, and whether cluster autoscaler or Karpenter is expected to add capacity.\n\n## Tradeoff\nReducing requests may make the Pod schedule, but it can also hide real capacity needs and cause runtime saturation later.","body_traditional":"## Pending means not placed or not ready to start\nA Pending Pod may be unscheduled, waiting for volumes, blocked by image pulls, or constrained by node selectors, taints, or resource requests. The scheduler tells you a lot in events.\n- Insufficient CPU or memory means requests do not fit current nodes.\n- Untolerated taint means the Pod is not allowed on matching nodes.\n- Node selector or affinity can make the Pod too picky.\n- PVC binding can block stateful Pods before the container starts.\n\n## Inspection commands\n$ kubectl describe pod pending-pod -n payments\n$ kubectl get nodes\n$ kubectl describe node NODE_NAME\n$ kubectl get pvc -n payments\nLook for `0/3 nodes are available`, taints, node affinity, volume binding, and whether cluster autoscaler or Karpenter is expected to add capacity.\n\n## Tradeoff\nReducing requests may make the Pod schedule, but it can also hide real capacity needs and cause runtime saturation later.","pinyin":"Scenario: A deployment rollout is stuck because new Pods stay Pending.\nChecklist:\n- Read the exact scheduler event message.\n- Compare Pod requests with allocatable node capacity.\n- Check taints, tolerations, node selectors, and PVCs.\n- Decide whether to scale capacity or correct workload constraints.\nCommands:\n$ kubectl describe pod pending-pod -n payments\n$ kubectl get nodes -o wide","audio_url":null,"video_url":null,"course_slug":"platform-kubectl-debugging-basics","course_category":"kubectl","course_era":"Platform Academy","vocabulary":[{"id":81,"simplified":"Pending","traditional":"Pending","pinyin":"Pod phase","definition":"A Pod phase before all containers are running and ready."},{"id":82,"simplified":"Taint","traditional":"Taint","pinyin":"Node restriction","definition":"A node marker that repels Pods without a matching toleration."},{"id":83,"simplified":"Toleration","traditional":"Toleration","pinyin":"Pod permission","definition":"A Pod setting that allows scheduling onto nodes with matching taints."},{"id":84,"simplified":"Node selector","traditional":"Node selector","pinyin":"Placement","definition":"A simple label-based requirement for node placement."},{"id":85,"simplified":"PVC","traditional":"PVC","pinyin":"Storage request","definition":"A PersistentVolumeClaim requesting storage for a Pod."}],"flashcards":[{"id":49,"lesson_id":22,"prompt":"Where do you find scheduler reasons for Pending Pods?","answer":"In `kubectl describe pod` events.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":50,"lesson_id":22,"prompt":"What does insufficient memory usually compare?","answer":"Pod memory requests against allocatable node memory.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":51,"lesson_id":22,"prompt":"Why not simply lower resource requests to schedule?","answer":"It can hide real capacity needs and create runtime saturation.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"}]}
